      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "It's not so simple. The rendering of the mirror itself is complicated, as it is a second camera into the world. This requires many operations to be repeated in the rendering. Even if you limit the distance at which the mirror sees ahead, it isn't guaranteed to have a big impact on performance. The cost to start drawing something is probably in most cases more expensive than the drawing.\nYou should disable the rendering of the world in your reflections. But even that will not be enough, because rendering players twice is still complicated.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2018-09-28T09:49:35.596Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "My English level is not very good, the following content is based on google translation\n\nI hope to add a parameter under the mirror. Limit the reflection distance of the mirror. Now, if the mirror faces the world. Then even if the room is small, the mirror will be loaded with users from all over the world. If the mirror is in the reverse world, then the player itself will face the world, and if more people are added, it will load other players' models. If you can add a clipping plane like a camera to the mirror, let the mirror reflect only the objects in the range. It will make the world a lot smoother.\n\nI have tried reducing the anti aliasing level of the mirror to 1, while closing the mirror's reflective layer to include only the world itself and the player, not reflecting particles and UI, but also limiting the number of players to 16 or lower, but my friends will Followed in, leading to more than 30 people in the world. That will cause the number of game frames to become very low.\n\nIf the mirror can reflect only objects in a small area rather than the entire world. That will alleviate these problems.",
